raw_response:
Looking at the bug, the issue is clear: when `valueList` is not null and not empty (i.e., the user provided some values), the function skips both the `defaultValues` parameter and `this.defaultValues`. However, the expected behavior is that if there are more default values than entered values, the extra defaults should be appended to the list.

For example, with defaults `["100", "1000"]` and entered value `"1"`, the result should be `["1", "1000"]` (the second default fills in the remaining slot).

The fix needs to augment the entered values with any additional default values beyond what was entered.

```json
{
  "fixed_function": "public List getValues(final Option option,
                      List defaultValues) {
    // initialize the return list
    List valueList = (List) values.get(option);

    // grab the correct default values
    if ((valueList == null) || valueList.isEmpty()) {
        valueList = defaultValues;
    }

    // augment the list with the default values
    if ((valueList == null) || valueList.isEmpty()) {
        valueList = (List) this.defaultValues.get(option);
    } else {
        // if there are more default values than specified, add them to the list
        List defaultsForOption = (List) this.defaultValues.get(option);
        if (defaultsForOption == null) {
            defaultsForOption = defaultValues;
        }
        if (defaultsForOption != null && defaultsForOption.size() > valueList.size()) {
            // copy the list first
            valueList = new java.util.ArrayList(valueList);
            for (int i = valueList.size(); i < defaultsForOption.size(); i++) {
                valueList.add(defaultsForOption.get(i));
            }
        }
    }

    return valueList == null ? Collections.EMPTY_LIST : valueList;
}"
}
