Looking at the bug, the issue is in the condition that decides whether to add a default year field. The condition checks if the first saved field's duration is between days and months (inclusive), which is meant to detect when only month/week fields are present without a year.

The current condition:
```java
if (compareReverse(first, months) >= 0 && compareReverse(first, days) <= 0) {
```

`compareReverse` returns negative if first > second. So `compareReverse(first, months) >= 0` means `first <= months`, and `compareReverse(first, days) <= 0` means `first >= days`. This detects fields with duration between days and months.

The problem is that when parsing with week-of-month (`ww`) combined with month (`MM`) and year (`yyyy` or `xxxx`), the year IS already saved, but the condition only checks the first field. When the first field is "weeks" (duration between days and months), it saves the default year again and recurses — but on the recursive call, the year field from the pattern is also there, causing the year to be set to `iDefaultYear` instead of the parsed year.

The fix should not add the default year if a year field is already saved. We need to check whether a year-type field has already been saved before adding the default year.

