This returns `1` whenever either `|u| <= 1` or `|v| <= 1`. But this is wrong - it should only return `1` if either `|u| == 1` or `|v| == 1` (i.e., one of them is exactly 1 or -1, meaning GCD must be 1). If either is `0`, GCD should be the other value. If both are `> 1`, we should proceed with the algorithm.

The original comment says "if either operand is abs 1, return 1". The condition `<= 1` also catches `0`, but for `0`, GCD(0, n) = n, so returning `1` is wrong.

More importantly, the case that breaks `Fraction(Integer.MIN_VALUE, 2)` is: when computing GCD(Integer.MIN_VALUE, 2), `Math.abs(Integer.MIN_VALUE)` overflows to `Integer.MIN_VALUE` which is negative, so `<= 1` is true, and we return `1` instead of the correct GCD of `2`.

The fix should change `<=` to `==` so that we only return 1 when either value is exactly `±1`:

```java
if (Math.abs(u) == 1 || Math.abs(v) == 1) {
    return 1;
}
```

But we also need to handle `0`: GCD(0, v) = v and GCD(u, 0) = u. However, looking at the calling code context, zero numerators are handled before calling GCD. The key fix is changing `<=` to `==`.

```json
{
  "fixed_function": "private static int greatestCommonDivisor(int u, int v) {
    // From Commons Math:
    //if either operand is abs 1, return 1:
    if (Math.abs(u) == 1 || Math.abs(v) == 1) {
        return 1;
    }
    // keep u and v negative, as negative integers range down to
    // -2^31, while positive numbers can only be as large as 2^31-1
    // (i.e. we can't necessarily negate a negative number without
    // overflow)
    if (u>0) { u=-u; } // make u negative
    if (v>0) { v=-v; } // make v negative
    // B1. [Find power of 2]
    int k=0;
    while ((u&1)==0 && (v&1)==0 && k<31) { // while u and v are both even...
        u/=2; v/=2; k++; // cast out twos.
    }
    if (k==31) {
        throw new ArithmeticException("overflow: gcd is 2^31");
    }
    // B2. Initialize: u and v have been divided by 2^k and at least
    //     one is odd.
    int t = ((u&1)==1) ? v : -(u/2)/*B3*/;
    // t negative: u was odd, v may be even (t replaces v)
    // t positive: u was even, v is odd (t replaces u)
    do {
        /* assert u<0 && v<0; */
        // B4/B3: cast out twos from t.
        while ((t&1)==0) { // while t is even..
            t/=2; // cast out twos
        }
        // B5 [reset max(u,v)]
        if (t>0) {
            u = -t;
        } else {
            v = t;
        }
        // B6/B3. at this point both u and v should be odd.
        t = (v - u)/2;
        // |u| larger: t positive (replace u)
        // |v| larger: t negative (replace v)
    } while (t!=0);
    return -u*(1<<k); // gcd is u*2^k
}"
}
