The fix should use the tag's name that respects the current parse settings. Looking at the code, `normalName()` always returns lowercase, but we need to use the name as it appears in the token (which respects case settings). We should use `t.asEndTag().name()` instead of `t.asEndTag().normalName()` for the comparison variable, but we also need to ensure the stack comparison works correctly.

Actually, the real issue is that `name` is set to `normalName()` (always lowercase), but `node.nodeName()` returns the case-preserved name when case preservation is enabled. So the comparison `node.nodeName().equals(name)` fails when the tag is uppercase like `X`.

The fix is to use `t.asEndTag().name()` instead of `t.asEndTag().normalName()` so that the name respects the case settings:

