issue_title:
Problem with Object id handling, explicit `null` token

raw_response:
Looking at the error, the issue is that when the JSON contains `'id':null`, the `StringDeserializer` throws an exception because it can't deserialize a `null` token into a `String`. The fix should handle null tokens before attempting deserialization by using `deserializeWithType` or by checking if the current token is null first.

The bug is that `_valueDeserializer.deserialize(p, ctxt)` is called even when the token is `VALUE_NULL`, and the `StringDeserializer` doesn't handle null tokens gracefully. We need to check for a null token first and return the instance directly (similar to what the comment says - null id should be allowed).

```json
{
  "fixed_function": "@Override
    public Object deserializeSetAndReturn(JsonParser p,
    		DeserializationContext ctxt, Object instance) throws IOException
    {
        if (p.getCurrentToken() == com.fasterxml.jackson.core.JsonToken.VALUE_NULL) {
            return instance;
        }
        Object id = _valueDeserializer.deserialize(p, ctxt);
        /* 02-Apr-2015, tatu: Actually, as per [databind#742], let it be;
         *  missing or null id is needed for some cases, such as cases where id
         *  will be generated externally, at a later point, and is not available
         *  quite yet. Typical use case is with DB inserts.
         */
        // note: no null checks (unlike usually); deserializer should fail if one found
        if (id == null) {
            return null;
        }
        ReadableObjectId roid = ctxt.findObjectId(id, _objectIdReader.generator, _objectIdReader.resolver);
        roid.bindItem(instance);
        // also: may need to set a property value as well
        SettableBeanProperty idProp = _objectIdReader.idProperty;
        if (idProp != null) {
            return idProp.setAndReturn(instance, id);
        }
        return instance;
    }"
}
